A||||14||MCFIEROGUY||Crank bearings||||1||
Z||000000||MCFIEROGUY||11-11-2000||10:00 PM||||How hard is it to replace my crank and rod bearings ?<BR>86 SE V-6  <P>------------------<BR>-Chris||||reg||1
Z||000001||rockcrawl||11-11-2000||10:16 PM||||If you are doing it in the car, you need to either unbolt and lift the engine, or notch the motor mount to get the oil pan off. You need to remove starter. After that, it's just like doing it on an engine stand except you have to deal with the occasional oil drip on your face! If a bearing has already spun and you have a knock, plan on pulling the engine and replacing the crankshaft.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||11-11-2000||11:26 PM||||Replacing crank bearings with the engine in the car would be next to, if not, impossible. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||11-12-2000||12:35 AM||||1fst2m6 I think did this to his car. You may want to consult him if he does not read this post.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||11-12-2000||10:11 AM||||1fst2m6 replaced the rod bearings on his engine with the engine in the car.||||reg||1
Z||000005||MCFIEROGUY||11-12-2000||01:58 PM||||so what your saying is that it is not possible to replace the main bearings with the engine in the car???<P>------------------<BR>-Chris||||reg||1
Z||000006||Indiana_resto_guy||11-12-2000||04:18 PM||||McFieroguy,<P>What he's saying is that it's impossible to replace the crankshaft with the engine in the car. It's not impossible, but would prove to be very, very difficult and would be something that I would not recommend be done.||||reg||1
Z||000007||rockcrawl||11-12-2000||07:34 PM||||Both rod and main bearings can be replaced with the engine in the car. The crank does not have to be removed to replace the main bearings. The new bearing shells can be placed on the crank and slid around into place. It's really not that difficult. The worst part of the job is first getting the pan off, and then lying on your back working under the car with oil dripping in your face.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||11-12-2000||07:46 PM||||Replacing crank bearings with the engine in the car will be a _____, if even possible. The oil pump will have to be removed to take off one main cap, and the timing chain cover blocks removal of another main cap. I really don't see any way to replace the crank bearings with the engine in the car.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Indiana_resto_guy||11-12-2000||11:44 PM||||What I use to turn the upper shells out is a cotter pin about 2" in legnth at least .025 thick on each side. Bend it into a 'T' and use #2 main cap to finish fit to the crankshaft oil journal. Do them one at a time, insert the make shift 'key' into the lubrication opening and rotate the crankshaft till the old shell is out. Pay attention to which way you turn the crank because each shell has a lock lug. Turn the old shell out in the direction of the lug end out first, install in the oppisite direction. Of course you need to start the new shell prior to turning it back in. Yes the oil pump will need removed along with the timming case cover in '85 & '86 V-6 cars. As long as the timming cover is off replace the chain and install a new oil pump too. <P>Now the BIG ?, Why are you replacing the bearings? Low oil pressure? Rod knocking? What?||||reg||1
Z||000010||Cooter||11-13-2000||12:01 AM||||If low oil pressure is the problem, it is more than likely the cam bearings are worn out and replacing the crank/rod bearings is not going to help.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||1FST2M6||11-13-2000||09:10 AM||||cost me $5.93 and 5 hours on the floor..  and about 1/2 bottle of clear eyes.. due to the oil and crap that drips DIRECTLY in your eyes! NEVER FAILS! pulled the nuts off the motor mount lifted the motor off the mount pulled the mount out dropped the 10 or so oil pan bolts and started taking the caps off.. make sure you put the cap in the SAME way you took it off.. they ARE deformed and will casue premature failure if you install them incorretly. re-install one bearing surface(rod end) and rotate the engine one revolution to make sure there is no binding. then move to the next one... it's NOT hard.. just time consuming. and make sure your hands are as clean as possible try and keep any/all grit/sand/dirt anything from the assembly lube and mating surface. if you take the bearing out and there is a pin broken a stainless pick will usually pop out the broken pin. you can use 400 grit emory cloth to clean up any abrasions from the crank. make sure you generously coat the bearing surface with assembly lube ($5 at NAPA) or a molybdenym grease. once you have the car all back together.. pull the coil wire OUT OF THE CAR! turn the car over a few times and watch the oil pressure guage once you have adaquit oil pressure go back install the coil wire and start the car.. POOF black smoke (unburnt fuel from turning i over with no spark) and your done!  the crank bearings cannot.. ok they can be done.. but NOT correctly. the timing chain and the tranny hold the crank in line. you CAN fight with it and HOPE that the new bearings line up and don't bind but i wouldn't try it.. the crank breaings are pretty tough and will take WAY more miles than the rod bearings...<P>------------------<BR>"Fondue forks for everybody"<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 11-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||rockcrawl||11-13-2000||10:00 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Replacing crank bearings with the engine in the car will be a _____, if even possible. The oil pump will have to be removed to take off one main cap, and the timing chain cover blocks removal of another main cap. I really don't see any way to replace the crank bearings with the engine in the car.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>The oil pump should be removed an replaced with a high volume unit anyway. The timing cover will not be a problem.<BR>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>the crank bearings cannot.. ok they can be done.. but NOT correctly. the timing chain and the tranny hold the crank in line. you CAN fight with it and HOPE that the new bearings line up and don't bind but i wouldn't try it..<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>The bearings hold the crank in line. Even with a new chain, there is <B>plenty</B> of play to allow the crank to drop down enough. The input shaft on the trans also has enough play. The front crank seal is what will limit how much the crank will drop, unless you forget to loosen the belts. Even if the crank only moves 1/100th of an inch, you would still be able to slide the new bearings into place.<P>with that said, and considering the engine is old enough to warrant bearing replacement, it would probably be worth while to just drop the cradle and rebuild the engine, or at least reseal it. Unless of course you're just trying to quiet the knock so you can sell it to some unsuspecting fool (of course I would never do that!).<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<p>[This message has been edited by rockcrawl (edited 11-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||MCFIEROGUY||11-14-2000||09:06 AM||||Thanks guys ....... I think although I am now just a little more confused than I was before, I think whatyour teying to tel me in your collective voice is that it is possible and may even be easy for some... but if you have to ask how to do it you should not do it.<BR>So I have decided to just replace the rod bearings and leave the rest for a total rebuild in the future .<BR>The reason I am doing this is what I believe is a rod knock however I have no real hard and fast evidence that that is what it is so it could be almost anything .<BR>Thanks for everyones help.<P>------------------<BR>-Chris||||reg||1
Z||000014||88formula||11-14-2000||09:43 PM||||I just want to add to 1FST2M6 post that when you take the coil wire off you have to ground it to the frame so you don't ruin the coil or spike your computer. You can unplug the fuel pump or pull the fuse for the injectors to prevent fuel buildup during cranking.||||reg||1
